From 100603c07c0203ed4b9c558bdd498dedce8c9d99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: chezjv <chezjv@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 23:15:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(video): adaptive bitrate driven by client loss
 reports (NVENC)

Lower the encoder bitrate while Moonlight reports packet loss and raise it
back, step by step, once the link has stayed clean, without interrupting the
stream.

Moonlight already sends Sunshine a per-frame FEC status (SS_FRAME_FEC_STATUS,
type 0x5502) for every frame that needed FEC recovery or was dropped, plus IDR
and reference frame invalidation requests when frames are lost. Those reports
now feed a small AIMD controller per session: a one second window with losses
cuts the bitrate (default 25 %, floor 20 % of the requested bitrate, 2 s
cooldown), a dropped frame cuts it right away, and after a configurable clean
period (default 8 s, doubling after each increase that caused losses) the
bitrate is raised again by 15 %, never above what the client requested.

The new target reaches the encoder through a mail event consumed by the encode
loop. The NVENC backend applies it with NvEncReconfigureEncoder(): only the
rate control parameters change, with no encoder reset and no forced IDR frame,
so the client only sees smaller or larger frames from the next frame on. Other
encoders ignore the request.

New configuration options: adaptive_bitrate (disabled by default),
adaptive_bitrate_min_percent, adaptive_bitrate_decrease_percent and
adaptive_bitrate_increase_delay.
